# Payload CMS

- Pin a supported Payload, Next.js, and React combination and verify APIs against that exact version.
- Use documented config, custom components/views, hooks, access controls, endpoints, and plugins.
- Never patch Payload internals, edit `node_modules`, or depend on undocumented DOM structure.
- Enforce access control on the server for collections, globals, custom endpoints, and dashboard queries.
- Treat Local API authorization as explicit: pass the user and disable access override where user permissions apply.
- Keep roles and permissions centralized, typed, deny-by-default, and covered by authorization tests.
- Use Payload drafts and versions for editorial state; do not recreate those mechanisms in parallel.
- Protect draft previews with short-lived authorization and prevent draft content from public responses and caches.
- Define schema changes through reviewed migrations with upgrade and rollback notes.
- Validate file type, size, ownership, alt text, and deletion references for media workflows.
- Keep the OJ module configurable for branding, routes, collection slugs, modules, support links, and site URL.
- Do not expose counts, recent items, or search results that include documents the current user cannot read.
- Preserve generated types and regenerate them whenever schemas change; CI must detect stale generated types.
